<h1>Windows CertUtil File Hash Guide for MD5 and SHA-256</h1>
<p>Windows includes a built-in command called certutil that can calculate file hashes. That makes it useful when you need to verify a downloaded file and do not want to install extra software. Certutil is often associated with certificates, but its -hashfile option is a practical checksum tool for normal users and administrators.</p>
<p>Microsoft's <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/certutil">certutil documentation</a> describes -hashfile as generating and displaying a cryptographic hash over a file. The command pattern is certutil -hashfile InFile HashAlgorithm. For example, certutil -hashfile installer.exe SHA256 calculates the SHA-256 hash of installer.exe.</p>
<p>You can also calculate MD5 when you need to compare against an older checksum: certutil -hashfile archive.zip MD5. Use this only when the publisher gives you an MD5 value or a legacy process requires it. For new verification, prefer SHA-256 because it is the stronger current choice for file checksums.</p>
<p>Run Command Prompt or PowerShell, move to the folder containing the file, and run the command. If the file path contains spaces, put it in quotation marks. For example, certutil -hashfile "C:\Users\Name\Downloads\big file.iso" SHA256. Most file-not-found errors come from a wrong path, missing quotes, or checking a file that is still downloading.</p>
<p>After certutil prints the hash, compare it with the publisher's expected value. Compare the full value, not only a short prefix. Copying into a plain text editor can make comparison easier. Watch for extra spaces, line breaks, or hidden characters when copying from websites or PDF files.</p>
<p>If the hash does not match, do not install the file. First check the algorithm, file version, and source page. Then download the file again from the official source. A mismatch may be harmless corruption, but it can also mean the wrong file or a tampered download. Treat it as a stop sign until you understand it.</p>
<p>Certutil is especially useful in February maintenance work, when teams clean old installers, drivers, ISO files, and exported archives before spring projects. Create a small manifest with file names and SHA-256 hashes for anything you need to keep. That makes future checks easier.</p>
<p>Certutil output may include labels and line breaks around the hash. When comparing values, copy only the hexadecimal hash or use a tool that compares strings cleanly. For automation, PowerShell Get-FileHash may be easier to parse, but certutil remains useful because it is widely available on Windows systems.</p>
<p>If certutil returns an error, check the path before assuming the file is bad. The command cannot hash a file it cannot find. Dragging the file into the terminal window can paste the full path on many Windows setups, which reduces typing mistakes with long download names.</p>
<p>Before putting this advice into production, write down the exact use case and risk level. File integrity, password storage, legacy compatibility, and message authentication are different problems. A clear note in the documentation prevents a future developer from copying the same hash choice into a place where it no longer fits.</p>
<p>Certutil is not a malware scanner and does not prove that software is safe. It only tells you whether the file matches a known digest. Use it as one part of a safer download process, along with official sources, signatures where available, and normal endpoint protection. For cross-platform commands, see <a href="/verify-file-checksums-windows-mac-linux.html">How to Verify File Checksums on Windows, macOS, and Linux</a>.</p>
